    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0dd681fd_b74d5c57",
        "filename": "fs/namei.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 825,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124848
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-19T17:47:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I meant d_path returns -ENAMETOOLONG (or similar) if buf is not big enough.\n\nThis is a bit of a limitation here. This buffer is on the stack, we could do dynamic allocation at a higher cost but have to handle failures and still is not much use since:\n1. The tracepoint code (in the .h part of this CL) copies the buf into the trace buffer which is a fixed size record (we could dynamically allocate strings in the trace buffer but eBPF doesn\u0027t support that AFAIK).\n2. Worst case size of full path IIRC is actually 4KB which is way too long and wasteful of trace buffer\u0027s space (each record will be 4K whether we have a path that big or not).",
      "parentUuid": "08127f5c_d0082934",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 825,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 825,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "546f478ff0a8464b6cc1fb3ae03b981a476c32fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "69e5b157_3540418c",
        "filename": "fs/namei.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 825,
      "author": {
        "id": 1130394
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-19T17:59:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I meant d_path returns -ENAMETOOLONG (or similar) if buf is not big enough\n\nErr, yeah. I read that too fast. It looks like we\u0027re truncating on mangling overflow.\n\n\u003e we could dynamically allocate strings in the trace buffer but eBPF doesn\u0027t support that AFAIK\n\nWhy not let eBPF truncate if desired? In general, I want to preserve all system information instead of imposing an arbitrary limit, as the constant-width field does. Besides: isn\u0027t 128 bytes also wasteful in many cases?",
      "parentUuid": "0dd681fd_b74d5c57",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 825,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 825,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "546f478ff0a8464b6cc1fb3ae03b981a476c32fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f28d9052_ae1d74cb",
        "filename": "fs/namei.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 825,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124848
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-19T18:15:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "128 can certainly be bumped up if it is too less. Right now in my testing on Android, I don\u0027t hit this limit. Even if eBPF truncates, some people (Igor\u0027s team) enable this in ftrace directly so that would consume trace buffer space (128 bytes per record). But, we can certainly revisit the limit later, if it turns out to be a problem.",
      "parentUuid": "69e5b157_3540418c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 825,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 825,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "546f478ff0a8464b6cc1fb3ae03b981a476c32fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "11ec3b0c_5574bacf",
        "filename": "fs/namei.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 825,
      "author": {
        "id": 1130394
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-19T18:21:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We\u0027re probably going to live with this facility for a long time, and it\u0027s probably (because it\u0027s useful) going to grow outside Android. It\u0027s not than 128 is too big or too small: it\u0027s that any arbitrary cap based on what we happen to hit today is going to cause pain later on, especially if the cap has runtime effects. Imagine an application developer experiencing inexplicable app startup slowdowns (due to iorapd resolution failure) just because he happens to expand the name of some file and push the total path over the 128-character limit. We should avoid these sorts of cliffs.",
      "parentUuid": "f28d9052_ae1d74cb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 825,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 825,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "546f478ff0a8464b6cc1fb3ae03b981a476c32fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
